WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:03.370
(authoritative music)

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.808 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and joining me today

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:11.778 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
is a professor of economics
at Brown University,

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:13.246 line:0%
a distinguished fellow

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:15.548 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the American
Economics Association,

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:19.586 align:start size:97% position:3%
and a member of the U.S.
Council on Foreign Relations,

00:00:19.586 --> 00:00:20.520
Glenn Loury.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.522
Welcome to "The Rubin Report."

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.290 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Thanks, Dave.
Good to be with you.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:25.492
- How'd I do on the bio there?

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:27.694
You have quite an extensive bio.

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:28.661
I've got the whole thing here.

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:30.263
I left off like 10 things

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:31.898 align:start size:88% position:13%
that probably could have
been the lead thing.

00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:33.333
- Well, I'm a member-
- Was that all right?

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:36.036 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- I'm a member of the American
Philosophical Society.

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:37.303 line:0%
I'm kind of proud of that

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:38.605 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
because those are
the guys that write,

00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:40.673 line:0%
and the girls, that
write the big books,

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.809 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I don't know why they
included me in their number.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.077
I haven't written any big books,

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:47.380 align:start size:84% position:16%
but the American
Philosophical Society.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.648
- All right, there you go,

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:50.050
so before we get
to anything else,

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.485
I wanna ask you about that shirt

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:55.889 align:start position:0%
'cause you flashed me something
right before we started,

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:57.490
straight outta quarantine.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:00.160
Are you a free man in a
free country right now?

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.097 align:start size:97% position:3%
- (chuckles) Well, I have
contracted, been treated for,

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:07.333 align:start size:97% position:3%
and recovered from COVID-19,
knock on wood, thank God,

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:12.105
so yeah, I mean, it's a,

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:13.606 align:start size:81% position:19%
my wife bought
this shirt for me.

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:15.108
It's kind of a play

00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:19.145
on the old hip-hop rappers,
"Straight Outta Compton,"

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:20.613
so I'm straight outta quarantine

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:22.816
and glad to be free at last.

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:24.250
- (chuckles) Free at last.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.752 align:start size:81% position:19%
Well, actually,
let's start there.

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.087
I wasn't planning
on doing too much

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:28.788 align:start size:84% position:16%
kind of COVID-related
stuff to you,

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:32.358 align:start size:88% position:13%
but what do you make
out of what has happened

00:01:32.358 --> 00:01:35.161
in the last year related
to COVID and lockdowns?

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:36.429
And then, I think some of that

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:37.697
will probably get us into

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:39.399
some of the social
issues of the day,

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:41.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is what you're
talking about a bit more.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.069
- Treacherous waters

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.370
that you're inviting
me into there, Dave.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.539
You could say the wrong
thing and get canceled.

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.608
I mean, a number of things

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:50.076
come to mind.
- Don't worry.

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:51.377 align:start size:97% position:3%
Nobody's been canceled 'cause
of me. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:54.147
- (laughs) I'm a
social scientist,

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:55.682
so the first thing
I think about is

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:56.983
what are the kinds of studies

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.719 align:start size:81% position:19%
that people are
gonna do for years

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:03.089
assessing what happened here?

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:04.491
And there are many
dimensions to it.

00:02:04.491 --> 00:02:06.092 align:start size:88% position:13%
There's the public
health dimension to it.

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:07.927 align:start size:84% position:16%
There was a
pandemic, a pathogen.

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:10.263 align:start size:88% position:13%
It spread in the
population. People died.

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:14.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
How effective were the
various responses to that:

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:17.337
shutdowns, masking,
and stuff like that,

00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:20.240
but I think there are also
some political things here.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.744 align:start size:97% position:3%
For example, Donald Trump
was hated by a lot of people.

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:26.112
They hung this around his neck.

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:27.947
Now, maybe he didn't
handle it as well...

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.449
I think I'll retract the maybe.

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:30.683
He didn't handle it

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:32.519 align:start size:84% position:16%
as well as he could
have handled it, okay?

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:33.753
On the other hand,

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:36.055
here was an opportunity
to sink Donald Trump,

00:02:36.055 --> 00:02:37.590
and I think a lot of
people seized on it,

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.894 align:start size:97% position:3%
so one of the interesting
political questions to me is

00:02:40.894 --> 00:02:43.596 align:start size:97% position:3%
how did the partisan
character of American politics

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:45.832
and the role the
media plays in that

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:48.434 align:start size:88% position:13%
affect the way that
this thing was reported?

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.170 align:start size:84% position:16%
But there are many,
many dimensions to it,

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:53.640 align:start size:84% position:16%
so the social
pressure to conform,

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:54.941
you know, have you
been vaccinated?

00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:56.476
And I'm for getting vaccinated.

00:02:56.476 --> 00:02:58.945
I've been vaccinated myself.

00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:00.547
I'm not against
being vaccinated.

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:03.716
Tucker Carlson, whom I
follow from time to time,

00:03:03.716 --> 00:03:05.418
is on a tear about
the masking stuff,

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:07.120 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there is a real
question there.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.422 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, the government
is gonna tell me

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:11.724
what I have to put on my face.

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:15.528 align:start size:97% position:3%
That works maybe in an
absolute emergency situation,

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:17.263 align:start size:84% position:16%
but when that becomes
a way of life,

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:18.698
you have to start
asking the question

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:20.900
about individual
freedom and so on,

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.869
so there's just many
different pieces to it

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:26.539 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I expect will be
examined by serious people

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:27.941
for years to come.

00:03:27.941 --> 00:03:29.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, was there
anything that shocked you

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:31.945 align:start size:84% position:16%
just about the general
way, not the media,

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:35.014 align:start size:84% position:16%
but just the way
people sort of became

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:37.984
so hyper-partisan about a virus,

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:41.354 align:start size:97% position:3%
meaning that the mask has
become sort of I'm a Democrat;

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:43.423
the non-mask is
I'm a Republican,

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:46.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
something like that,
sort of loosely speaking.

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:48.027
- Well, that's one aspect of it.

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:51.664
I mean, and some people were
just absolutely terrified.

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, they wouldn't
come out of their houses.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.534
They had their groceries,

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:57.270 align:start size:88% position:13%
they would wipe down
everything with a cloth,

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.306
and I thought that
was over the top.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:01.507
I thought that was excessive,

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.276
but then, how do we handle risk?

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:05.578 align:start size:88% position:13%
Everybody is gonna
react to it differently,

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:07.714 align:start size:84% position:16%
so what I was saying
to my friends was

00:04:07.714 --> 00:04:10.650
if you told me there was
a one-in-a-thousand chance

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.286 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I would die if I
stepped from my front door,

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:17.557
I might stay inside for
a week, maybe two weeks.

00:04:17.557 --> 00:04:20.260 align:start size:97% position:3%
After that, I take my
chances. You know what I mean?

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:24.998
So don't impose your
risk preference on me.

00:04:24.998 --> 00:04:26.833
That was kind of my...

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:28.501 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I'm like, "It
takes two to tango,"

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.003 align:start size:81% position:19%
so if I wanna have
a dinner party,

00:04:30.003 --> 00:04:32.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the people don't wanna
show up, what can I do?

00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:34.974
But I did feel

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:37.844 align:start size:84% position:16%
that there was a
lot of over-the-top
kind of overreaction

00:04:37.844 --> 00:04:39.312
to schools staying
closed and whatnot.

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:41.581
I'm not an expert on the
data about school closings,

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:44.284
but my colleague Emily Oster is,

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:46.386
at Brown University's
economics department.

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:48.888
Look her up. She had a piece
of "The New Yorker" about this.

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:50.390
She is an expert.
She's a liberal.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:54.260 align:start size:97% position:3%
She's a liberal, and they
just tried to tear her apart

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.729 align:start size:84% position:16%
because she said
schools should open up

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.598
because she said the
data don't justify

00:04:58.598 --> 00:05:00.199
keeping the kids away
from the classroom.

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:01.801
You're doing more harm
than you're doing good.

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.569
The cure is worse
than the disease.

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:05.838
Where did I hear that
before? She said it.

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:08.808 align:start size:97% position:3%
She said it, and they
tried to take her head off,

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:11.577
so it was really,

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:15.982
really a very interesting and
very pathological situation

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:19.018 align:start size:97% position:3%
from the way in which we
made decisions as a society.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:23.890 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think politics, fear,
panic, groupthink,

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:26.092
these were all at play.

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:28.895
- What do you consider
yourself politically?

00:05:28.895 --> 00:05:31.364
Do you specifically say,

00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:33.299
"I'm this, I'm that,
I'm the other thing"?

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:34.534
'Cause when I'm
listening to you,

00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:36.202
I sense a little
bit of everything,

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:37.403
which, by the way,

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:38.504 align:start size:88% position:13%
that's probably how
most people really are.

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:39.605
We just get hung up

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:40.940 align:start size:72% position:28%
on the labels.
- Okay,

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.309
so I'm a registered independent.

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:45.745
I don't tell people how I vote.

00:05:45.745 --> 00:05:47.146
What I was telling them

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:48.581 align:start size:94% position:6%
during last year's
presidential election is,

00:05:48.581 --> 00:05:50.383
"Of course I voted for Biden.

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:51.984
"What else would you
expect me to say?"

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:53.219
And then I'd laugh,

00:05:53.219 --> 00:05:54.587
and I'd say, "I can't
say anything else."

00:05:54.587 --> 00:05:56.289 align:start size:84% position:16%
You still don't
know who I voted for

00:05:56.289 --> 00:05:57.557
if I tell you I voted for Biden

00:05:57.557 --> 00:05:58.925
'cause if I voted
for the other guy,

00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:00.760
I'd never tell you
I voted for him,

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:03.196
but I'm a conservative.

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:05.631
I think I just have
to admit that now

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:07.700 align:start size:84% position:16%
on a number of
different dimensions.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.536
I'm an economist, and
I lean neoliberal.

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.672 align:start size:88% position:13%
I lean toward open
markets, and free trade,

00:06:12.672 --> 00:06:14.674 align:start size:84% position:16%
and letting the
prices do their work.

00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:16.376
I don't like the Green New Deal

00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:20.680
because these people wanna
absolutely run our economy

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.217 align:start position:0%
on behalf of their vision about
how the future should look,

00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:25.618
and that terrifies me.

00:06:25.618 --> 00:06:28.654 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think taxes are, generally
speaking, too high.

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:33.659
I think regulation can go,
often does go over the top

00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:37.263
and impedes our prosperity.

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:38.998 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think the way
forward has been shown

00:06:38.998 --> 00:06:41.401
by capitalism with a C,

00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:44.771 align:start size:84% position:16%
capitalism not with
a K, et cetera,

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:47.240 align:start size:94% position:6%
so that makes me somewhat
of a conservative.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.742 align:start size:84% position:16%
I write a lot about
racial issues,

00:06:49.742 --> 00:06:52.211 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I'm cutting against
the grain there.

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:53.813
I think the country
has lost its mind

00:06:53.813 --> 00:06:55.281
with this diversity
and inclusion stuff.

00:06:55.281 --> 00:06:57.350
I'm not a big fan of
affirmative action.

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:59.719
I think reparations would
be an absolute disaster

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:01.421
for this country as well
as for African Americans.

00:07:01.421 --> 00:07:03.322
We could talk about it,

00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:04.590
so they call me a conservative,

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.159
and I'm not fighting
with them about it.

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:07.393
- Yeah, I hear you,

00:07:07.393 --> 00:07:09.162
and I think what you
just said right there

00:07:09.162 --> 00:07:13.266 align:start position:0%
sums up what most of my audience
probably is at some level,

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:15.701
whether some are maybe a
little more libertarian,

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.537 align:start size:97% position:3%
or a little more
traditionally conservative,

00:07:17.537 --> 00:07:18.805
or whatever it is,

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:20.540
but you just gave me
a great sort of lead

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.074
on everything else
I wanna talk about

00:07:22.074 --> 00:07:24.644
because one of the things
that I wanna talk about is

00:07:24.644 --> 00:07:28.014 align:start size:97% position:3%
we hear this phrase systemic
racism all the time,

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:29.348
and I've heard you
talk about this.

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:31.150
I've read some of
your stuff on this.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.585
In some ways,

00:07:32.585 --> 00:07:35.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're just not all talking
about the same thing

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:37.890 align:start size:84% position:16%
when we say the phrase
systemic racism,

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.927
so can you lay out maybe
what you believe it to mean

00:07:40.927 --> 00:07:44.197
versus what you think some
other people think it means.

00:07:44.197 --> 00:07:45.898
- Okay, in the simplest terms,

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:47.567
here's what I think
people are saying.

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:49.035 align:start size:84% position:16%
They're saying back
in the old days

00:07:49.035 --> 00:07:51.838 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the civil rights movement,
Civil Rights Act of 1964,

00:07:51.838 --> 00:07:53.806
Martin Luther King,
and all of that,

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:57.477
the issue was racist treatment,
unequal treatment of people,

00:07:57.477 --> 00:08:00.346
discrimination in
housing, and jobs,

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:02.648
and credit markets, and so on.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:03.850
Blacks weren't treated the same.

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:05.651
They were discriminated against,

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:07.220
and the goal was
equal treatment,

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.455
which is why King
is there saying,

00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:11.257
"I have a dream that
my kids will be judged

00:08:11.257 --> 00:08:12.558
"by the content of
their character."

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:14.260
He doesn't want them
discriminated against.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.596
He doesn't want them kept from
voting 'cause they're black.

00:08:16.596 --> 00:08:17.997
He doesn't want them
kept from shopping

00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:19.432
because they're black.

00:08:19.432 --> 00:08:20.967 align:start size:94% position:6%
He wants them to be
treated like anybody else.

00:08:20.967 --> 00:08:23.202
That's the old school thing.

00:08:23.202 --> 00:08:27.106 align:start size:97% position:3%
Systemic racism is declaring
there to be a new day

00:08:27.106 --> 00:08:28.975
because, guess what?

00:08:28.975 --> 00:08:32.278
We pretty much have solved
the discrimination problem.

00:08:32.278 --> 00:08:35.381 align:start size:88% position:13%
Not completely, but
just about as completely

00:08:35.381 --> 00:08:38.251
as you could solve
it in a free society.

00:08:38.251 --> 00:08:41.354 align:start size:88% position:13%
Every state has an
Equal Employment
Opportunity Commission.

00:08:41.354 --> 00:08:42.622
There's a ton of lawsuits

00:08:42.622 --> 00:08:44.290
that you can bring
against your employer

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.292
if you think you're not
being treated fairly.

00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:49.495
God help a retailer if he
actually does discriminate

00:08:49.495 --> 00:08:52.498
and charge a different price
to a black customer, right?

00:08:52.498 --> 00:08:53.432
So-

00:08:53.432 --> 00:08:54.867
- Which we would all be against,

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:55.868 align:start size:97% position:3%
just to be very clear.
- Yeah, we're against that.

00:08:55.868 --> 00:08:57.837
That bit has been solved,

00:08:57.837 --> 00:09:01.440 align:start size:84% position:16%
but the disparities
are still there, okay?

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:04.477
Blacks are overrepresented
amongst the incarcerated,

00:09:04.477 --> 00:09:08.147
underrepresented amongst the
students at a fancy college,

00:09:08.147 --> 00:09:10.583 align:start size:97% position:3%
have lower wealth holdings,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:09:10.583 --> 00:09:13.152
so the disparities are there,

00:09:13.152 --> 00:09:15.221 align:start size:81% position:19%
but the account
of the disparities

00:09:15.221 --> 00:09:17.757
that black person was
discriminated against

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:22.061 align:start size:97% position:3%
is no longer very effective
as an explanation,

00:09:22.061 --> 00:09:24.997 align:start size:84% position:16%
so people are calling
on larger forces,

00:09:24.997 --> 00:09:29.435 align:start size:88% position:13%
not personal, one-on-one
historical forces.

00:09:29.435 --> 00:09:32.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, here's an example:
New Orleans floods.

00:09:33.539 --> 00:09:37.643 align:start size:97% position:3%
Black people, on average, live
at lower level, sea level,

00:09:37.643 --> 00:09:39.779
so they get flooded worse.

00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:42.114
They get flooded worse
'cause they're poor.

00:09:42.114 --> 00:09:43.316
They get flooded worse,

00:09:43.316 --> 00:09:44.717
maybe because in the
history of New Orleans,

00:09:44.717 --> 00:09:46.118
I don't know anything
about it specifically,

00:09:46.118 --> 00:09:47.920
but this is just a hypothetical,

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.355
they were discriminated against,

00:09:49.355 --> 00:09:51.490 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the high-ground
housing wasn't as easily,

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.659
those neighborhoods weren't the,

00:09:53.659 --> 00:09:56.062 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
real estate agents didn't
show them, whatever, whatever.

00:09:56.062 --> 00:09:59.699 align:start position:0% line:0%
They got worse jobs, et cetera,
so they're on the lowland,

00:09:59.699 --> 00:10:01.701 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
so then the flood comes
and they're discriminated.

00:10:01.701 --> 00:10:04.136
Now, some of us would think

00:10:04.136 --> 00:10:05.905 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's an unfortunate
act of nature,

00:10:05.905 --> 00:10:08.407
and that, just like
if a pandemic comes,

00:10:08.407 --> 00:10:11.844
and you got more comorbidities
and pre-existing conditions,

00:10:11.844 --> 00:10:13.613
you're going to
get a harder hit,

00:10:13.613 --> 00:10:18.084 align:start size:97% position:3%
but it's not an act of
racism, a person might say,

00:10:18.084 --> 00:10:20.953 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the systemic racism people
wanna say, "Oh, no, no, no.

00:10:20.953 --> 00:10:24.156 align:start size:97% position:3%
"It is an act act of racism.
You see that disparity.

00:10:24.156 --> 00:10:25.591
"The history of race in America

00:10:25.591 --> 00:10:27.960 align:start size:84% position:16%
"is what has made that
disparity happen."

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:29.028
Then they have their story.

00:10:29.028 --> 00:10:30.496
In this case,

00:10:30.496 --> 00:10:33.099
the blacks are in the low-lying
neighborhoods in New Orleans

00:10:33.099 --> 00:10:36.369
because of discrimination
in the past, and racism,

00:10:36.369 --> 00:10:37.637
and so that's their story.

00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:40.172 align:start size:81% position:19%
Mass incarceration
is racist. Why?

00:10:40.172 --> 00:10:43.409
Because, well, the drug
laws are what they are,

00:10:43.409 --> 00:10:44.744
and they wouldn't
be what they are

00:10:44.744 --> 00:10:46.245 align:start size:84% position:16%
if the people who
were being locked up

00:10:46.245 --> 00:10:47.647
were mostly white people,

00:10:47.647 --> 00:10:49.348
and they have their accounts.

00:10:49.348 --> 00:10:51.817
There are not enough
blacks at Caltech. Why?

00:10:51.817 --> 00:10:53.185
Because of systemic racism

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:55.054
in the education
system, and so on,

00:10:55.054 --> 00:10:58.958
so it's an effort to
explain disparities

00:10:58.958 --> 00:11:00.426
in the absence

00:11:00.426 --> 00:11:03.896
of individual racist
discriminatory acts.

00:11:03.896 --> 00:11:05.097
That's how I understand it.

00:11:05.097 --> 00:11:06.565
- So I'm guessing,

00:11:06.565 --> 00:11:08.968
well, of course you know of and
probably know Thomas Sowell,

00:11:08.968 --> 00:11:11.404 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the book "Discrimination
Versus Disparities,"

00:11:11.404 --> 00:11:12.772
I mean, he goes into this a lot,

00:11:12.772 --> 00:11:15.941
so what do we do about
the disparity stuff?

00:11:15.941 --> 00:11:17.410 align:start size:94% position:6%
For those that understand
what you're saying,

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:19.145
they're saying, "Okay,
the system's not racist,"

00:11:19.145 --> 00:11:22.348
meaning there are no racist
laws against black people,

00:11:22.348 --> 00:11:26.585
but that wanna acknowledge
that these disparities exist.

00:11:26.585 --> 00:11:28.921
Where do you start
with the disparities?

00:11:28.921 --> 00:11:31.657
'Cause we seem, we
can't even get there.

00:11:31.657 --> 00:11:33.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, if you
follow Thomas Sowell,

00:11:33.125 --> 00:11:34.927
I do know him, not that well,

00:11:34.927 --> 00:11:36.162
although I've just joined

00:11:36.162 --> 00:11:38.230
as a senior fellow at
the Hoover Institution,

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:39.565
so I'm gonna get
to know him better.

00:11:39.565 --> 00:11:41.801 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'll add that to
the bio for next time.

00:11:41.801 --> 00:11:43.102
- I just signed on.

00:11:43.102 --> 00:11:45.638
I haven't even been, set
foot on the campus yet,

00:11:45.638 --> 00:11:48.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I'm looking forward to it.
- Nice, that's awesome.

00:11:48.674 --> 00:11:51.310 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But if you follow
Thomas Sowell,

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:54.980 align:start size:84% position:16%
he would say it's a
fool's errand to think

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:58.017
that you're ever gonna get
rid of all the disparities.

00:11:58.017 --> 00:12:01.287
He would say, "Look at the
ethnic Chinese in Indonesia,

00:12:01.287 --> 00:12:03.255
"and the Philippines,
and Southeast Asia."

00:12:03.255 --> 00:12:05.658
He says, "Look at them.
They're businesspeople.

00:12:05.658 --> 00:12:09.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
"They are wealthier. They
are better educated."

00:12:09.128 --> 00:12:10.863
He would say, "Look
at the Sinhalese

00:12:10.863 --> 00:12:14.266
"and the Tamil in Sri Lanka."

00:12:14.266 --> 00:12:17.236
He would say, "Look at the
Lebanese in West Africa."

00:12:17.236 --> 00:12:19.438
He would say, "Look at the
Jews pretty much everywhere."

00:12:19.438 --> 00:12:24.443
He would say, "There is
no absence of disparity."

00:12:24.443 --> 00:12:28.347
The story of ethnic
history is disparity,

00:12:28.347 --> 00:12:29.949
so if you set for yourself

00:12:29.949 --> 00:12:32.618
the goal of getting
rid of disparity,

00:12:32.618 --> 00:12:34.386
all right, this puts
it a little sharply,

00:12:34.386 --> 00:12:36.555
but I think it's not inaccurate,

00:12:36.555 --> 00:12:38.424
you're buying into tyranny

00:12:38.424 --> 00:12:40.359
because the only way
to get rid of disparity

00:12:40.359 --> 00:12:43.028
is to force things to be equal

00:12:43.028 --> 00:12:44.630
when the natural
state of affairs

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:46.532
would not have things be equal.

00:12:46.532 --> 00:12:49.268
What would getting rid of
disparity in the NBA mean?

00:12:49.268 --> 00:12:50.536
Or the NFL?

00:12:50.536 --> 00:12:51.837
I mean, well, know
what it would mean.

00:12:51.837 --> 00:12:53.405
It would mean a lot of
Chinese point guards,

00:12:53.405 --> 00:12:56.075 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it would be a
lot less interesting.

00:12:56.075 --> 00:12:57.309
That's a cheap shot, I know.

00:12:57.309 --> 00:12:58.778
I mean, that was
easy for me to say.

00:12:58.778 --> 00:13:00.179 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You're going to my weakness.
- But the point stands.

00:13:00.179 --> 00:13:01.447 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Man, I got a basketball
right behind me.

00:13:01.447 --> 00:13:03.883
45 with a blown-out
ACL in my left knee,

00:13:03.883 --> 00:13:06.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
but that's what I'd
rather be doing than this.

00:13:06.118 --> 00:13:07.520
- Oh, okay. You were good, huh?

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:09.321 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I was all right, yeah.
- In your day.

00:13:09.321 --> 00:13:12.124
- All right, so
Sowell would say,

00:13:12.124 --> 00:13:14.994
"Don't expect that there's
not gonna be disparities."

00:13:16.195 --> 00:13:20.232
I would agree with that,
but I would go further.

00:13:20.232 --> 00:13:22.134
I don't think that...

00:13:22.134 --> 00:13:23.903 align:start size:84% position:16%
If I don't like
disparities, get busy.

00:13:23.903 --> 00:13:26.839
This is what I wanna say to
my fellow African Americans.

00:13:26.839 --> 00:13:28.474
You don't like the disparities?

00:13:28.474 --> 00:13:30.976
You don't like the wealth
gap? Start a business.

00:13:30.976 --> 00:13:33.112
You don't think there's
enough black engineers?

00:13:33.112 --> 00:13:34.780
Okay, let's study some calculus.

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:37.449
Let's get some kids together.
We'll do it on the summer.

00:13:37.449 --> 00:13:38.584
We'll have a summer camp,

00:13:38.584 --> 00:13:40.386 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we'll do a
bootcamp on calculus.

00:13:40.386 --> 00:13:42.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
Kid that can do the
integral equation fastest

00:13:42.688 --> 00:13:45.558 align:start size:97% position:3%
will get a star on his thing,
and whatever, whatever.

00:13:45.558 --> 00:13:47.626
All right, get busy.

00:13:47.626 --> 00:13:50.262
Disparities don't
have to be destiny.

00:13:52.031 --> 00:13:53.933
Just because there's a disparity

00:13:53.933 --> 00:13:56.135 align:start size:94% position:6%
doesn't mean that we have
to accept it as given

00:13:56.135 --> 00:14:00.139
if, indeed, we want better for
ourselves and for our people,

00:14:00.139 --> 00:14:03.142 align:start size:88% position:13%
but don't be looking
outside of the community

00:14:03.142 --> 00:14:08.080
for society, or white
people, or whatever

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:09.615
to solve that problem for you,

00:14:09.615 --> 00:14:12.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
especially when our
families are in disarray,

00:14:12.785 --> 00:14:15.421 align:start size:88% position:13%
especially when the
behavior of some people,

00:14:15.421 --> 00:14:16.755
a few in our communities,

00:14:16.755 --> 00:14:18.557
makes life absolutely impossible

00:14:18.557 --> 00:14:20.659
for anybody else
to function there

00:14:20.659 --> 00:14:21.827
because of the violence,

00:14:21.827 --> 00:14:24.063 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the chaotic
behavior, and whatnot,

00:14:24.063 --> 00:14:26.498
especially when the
TV can be turned off

00:14:26.498 --> 00:14:29.602
and the kid could be directed
to do his homework, or hers.

00:14:29.602 --> 00:14:32.004 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's a part of the
parental responsibility,

00:14:32.004 --> 00:14:33.239
et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:33.239 --> 00:14:35.241
I don't mean to be
putting people down.

00:14:35.241 --> 00:14:38.010 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just mean to say our
fate, this is my argument,

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:40.913 align:start size:84% position:16%
is substantially
within our own hands,

00:14:40.913 --> 00:14:43.249
so if you don't like the
disparities, get busy.

00:14:43.249 --> 00:14:44.750
- Disparities aren't destiny.

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:47.720
That's a title for a book
right there. You got it.

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:50.356 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's the next book
you gotta write.

00:14:50.356 --> 00:14:53.459
Within all that, you're
talking about the family,

00:14:53.459 --> 00:14:56.528 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then Sowell
also talks about how

00:14:56.528 --> 00:14:59.031 align:start size:97% position:3%
when the welfare state
really kicked in in the '70s,

00:14:59.031 --> 00:15:01.967
that, in essence, it blew
apart the black family,

00:15:03.369 --> 00:15:06.505 align:start size:97% position:3%
but even talking about this,
people will not be kind.

00:15:07.773 --> 00:15:09.408
- Yeah, well, you've
got two things going.

00:15:09.408 --> 00:15:11.477
You got feminism,

00:15:11.477 --> 00:15:13.612 align:start size:84% position:16%
so what I mean is
you have a revolution

00:15:13.612 --> 00:15:17.650 align:start size:97% position:3%
in political-social attitudes
around the roles of women

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:21.287
and about the sort of legitimacy

00:15:21.287 --> 00:15:23.722 align:start size:88% position:13%
of conventional
two-parent arrangements
and stuff like that,

00:15:23.722 --> 00:15:26.058 align:start size:88% position:13%
so I don't wanna just
put it all on the women.

00:15:26.058 --> 00:15:28.294
The sexual morality
ideas have changed,

00:15:28.294 --> 00:15:29.762
gay rights and stuff like that.

00:15:29.762 --> 00:15:32.564 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, it's a different world
than it was 50 years ago,

00:15:32.564 --> 00:15:35.167
and that has worked
against, I think,

00:15:36.735 --> 00:15:41.040
the job of mending
the black family.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:42.241
I'd put it in those terms.

00:15:42.241 --> 00:15:45.110 align:start size:84% position:16%
People will get upset,
but what I mean is

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:49.114
if 70% of kids, babies
born to a black woman,

00:15:49.114 --> 00:15:50.616
are born to a woman
without a husband,

00:15:50.616 --> 00:15:55.087
this is not a healthy state
of affairs for the community.

00:15:55.087 --> 00:15:56.455
That has consequences

00:15:56.455 --> 00:15:59.758 align:start position:0%
in terms of the human
development of those youngsters.

00:15:59.758 --> 00:16:02.761 align:start size:88% position:13%
Boys need fathers.
Girls need fathers too.

00:16:02.761 --> 00:16:05.097 align:start size:88% position:13%
Adolescent boys with
the testosterone flowing

00:16:05.097 --> 00:16:05.931
need discipline.

00:16:05.931 --> 00:16:07.166
They need fathers.

00:16:07.166 --> 00:16:08.400
They need examples
of what it means

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.936
to live rightly as
a man in the world.

00:16:10.936 --> 00:16:12.438
In the absence of that,

00:16:12.438 --> 00:16:14.440 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're gonna get the kind
of chaos that you're seeing,

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:15.574
so you've got that,

00:16:15.574 --> 00:16:17.209
that the social liberals,

00:16:17.209 --> 00:16:21.013
the people who wanna
rewrite the rules,

00:16:21.013 --> 00:16:25.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
will object to invoking
tradition in this context.

00:16:25.451 --> 00:16:27.686
The other piece you've got is

00:16:27.686 --> 00:16:30.522 align:start size:88% position:13%
we had a war on poverty,
and poverty won.

00:16:30.522 --> 00:16:33.092 align:start size:88% position:13%
That would be, that's
what Charles Murray said

00:16:33.092 --> 00:16:35.427
in that classic book of
his, "Losing Ground."

00:16:35.427 --> 00:16:36.895
That's what Thomas
Sowell is saying

00:16:36.895 --> 00:16:40.065
when he points out the ill
effects of the welfare state,

00:16:40.065 --> 00:16:42.501 align:start size:97% position:3%
and on the other side of
that is the Democratic Party,

00:16:42.501 --> 00:16:44.703
and the liberal political
establishment, and whatnot,

00:16:44.703 --> 00:16:48.240 align:start size:97% position:3%
who believe that the transfers
from the welfare state

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:50.009
could do no harm.

00:16:50.009 --> 00:16:52.711
There are no incentive problems.

00:16:52.711 --> 00:16:54.680
Now, if you tell a woman

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:58.117
that she can get by on a...

00:16:58.117 --> 00:16:59.051
It's not a good living.

00:16:59.051 --> 00:17:00.519
Welfare is not a good living,

00:17:00.519 --> 00:17:03.389 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it is money
coming from the state,

00:17:03.389 --> 00:17:05.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you think that that
doesn't affect her behavior.

00:17:05.858 --> 00:17:08.360
Well, again, I'm an economist.

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:10.429
Of course, it does
affect her behavior,

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:14.733
so I don't know that
I wanna declare war

00:17:14.733 --> 00:17:17.970
on Medicaid or food stamps.

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:19.571
These are established programs.

00:17:19.571 --> 00:17:21.507
They're hundreds of
billions of dollars

00:17:21.507 --> 00:17:23.275
going out the federal door,

00:17:23.275 --> 00:17:25.744
but there are families
that need these benefits.

00:17:25.744 --> 00:17:28.313
I think we've gone far
enough down this road now

00:17:28.313 --> 00:17:30.983
that it's hard to
imagine going back,

00:17:30.983 --> 00:17:34.620 align:start size:84% position:16%
but if, as a
historical analysis,

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:37.089
I think part of the weight
has to be laid at the feet,

00:17:37.089 --> 00:17:41.160
the weight of the structure of
African American family life

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:42.961
in the year 2021

00:17:42.961 --> 00:17:47.466
has to be laid at the feet
of those who didn't listen

00:17:47.466 --> 00:17:50.235
when they were warned
about the ill effects

00:17:50.235 --> 00:17:53.972
of transfer programs on the
incentives that people had,

00:17:53.972 --> 00:17:55.474
so yeah.
- So yeah,

00:17:55.474 --> 00:17:57.543
so speaking of those
who didn't listen,

00:17:57.543 --> 00:17:58.944
you mentioned the
social liberals.

00:17:58.944 --> 00:18:01.346 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now, I'm guessing
at Brown University,

00:18:01.346 --> 00:18:04.049
politically, you
are an outsider.

00:18:04.049 --> 00:18:05.784
You're surrounded by
a lot of liberals,

00:18:05.784 --> 00:18:06.952
although I wouldn't say

00:18:06.952 --> 00:18:08.420 align:start size:88% position:13%
they're old school
liberals at this point.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:10.456 align:start position:0%
You're probably surrounded,
really, by a bunch of leftists,

00:18:10.456 --> 00:18:11.990
but do you find

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:15.594 align:start size:88% position:13%
that some of your old
school socially liberal,

00:18:15.594 --> 00:18:18.564 align:start size:94% position:6%
say, classical liberal
colleagues, if any remain,

00:18:18.564 --> 00:18:20.032
kind of on the DL are like,

00:18:20.032 --> 00:18:21.867 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Yeah, Glenn, you're right
about all this stuff,

00:18:21.867 --> 00:18:26.105
"but, you know, I don't want
them showing up at my house."

00:18:27.573 --> 00:18:32.578
- Yes, not quite those words,
but pretty much that attitude.

00:18:33.312 --> 00:18:34.813
A lot of people...

00:18:34.813 --> 00:18:36.348
I mean, there's a lot of
different stuff going on.

00:18:36.348 --> 00:18:38.817
Some people are religious,
and they're in the closet.

00:18:38.817 --> 00:18:41.487
They actually believe
in God. They pray.

00:18:41.487 --> 00:18:44.389
They read the Bible, and
they won't talk about it.

00:18:45.524 --> 00:18:46.992
Some people have misgivings.

00:18:46.992 --> 00:18:49.161
They're not sure, but they
know that the wind is blowing

00:18:49.161 --> 00:18:51.096
so strong in the
other direction,

00:18:51.096 --> 00:18:52.598
but I'll tell you,

00:18:52.598 --> 00:18:55.467
I've been encouraged by some
of my students here at Brown,

00:18:55.467 --> 00:18:59.104 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is notorious for being
an ultraliberal bastion,

00:18:59.104 --> 00:19:01.306
and certainly we've
got plenty of that,

00:19:01.306 --> 00:19:02.875 align:start size:84% position:16%
but there are
students around here

00:19:02.875 --> 00:19:06.178
who are hungry for
something different,

00:19:06.178 --> 00:19:09.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I've found them coming
to some of my classes,

00:19:09.648 --> 00:19:11.984
and coming around to
talk to me privately.

00:19:11.984 --> 00:19:14.586
I'm not saying that they would
have voted for Donald Trump.

00:19:14.586 --> 00:19:16.355
They would've never done that.

00:19:16.355 --> 00:19:17.823
I'm not saying that
they're pro-life.

00:19:17.823 --> 00:19:19.458
They're not; they're pro-choice.

00:19:19.458 --> 00:19:21.860
They're pro-gay marriage
and all that kind of stuff,

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:23.562
but I am saying that

00:19:23.562 --> 00:19:27.733
they find some of the concerns

00:19:27.733 --> 00:19:31.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I've been expressing
around these issues

00:19:31.403 --> 00:19:32.604
to resonate with them,

00:19:32.604 --> 00:19:34.406
and some of them are religious,

00:19:34.406 --> 00:19:36.575
and they will come,
and they will say,

00:19:36.575 --> 00:19:40.078 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I must say, I'm
not myself, personally,

00:19:40.078 --> 00:19:42.848
a professing Christian
at this point in my life.

00:19:42.848 --> 00:19:44.550
I used to be very strongly.

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:47.119
I was baptized at the age of 40.

00:19:47.119 --> 00:19:49.288
It's a long story. I'll
tell it in my memoir.

00:19:49.288 --> 00:19:53.225 align:start size:81% position:19%
Stay tuned, but
I'm open to people

00:19:55.427 --> 00:19:57.696 align:start size:88% position:13%
who are overt about
expressing their faith,

00:19:57.696 --> 00:20:01.133 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I have deep respect
for this challenge

00:20:01.133 --> 00:20:05.804
that we all face
as sentient beings

00:20:05.804 --> 00:20:09.441 align:start size:97% position:3%
of contemplating the ultimate
questions of existence,

00:20:09.441 --> 00:20:12.244
and there are no easy answers
to those ultimate questions,

00:20:12.244 --> 00:20:14.346 align:start size:84% position:16%
so I find that in
some of my students.

00:20:14.346 --> 00:20:15.914 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm just saying Brown
is not one thing.

00:20:15.914 --> 00:20:18.517 align:start size:84% position:16%
We have a reputation,
deservedly so,

00:20:18.517 --> 00:20:21.386
but it's a complicated and
interesting community here,

00:20:21.386 --> 00:20:23.655
and I do find like minds,

00:20:23.655 --> 00:20:26.291 align:start size:88% position:13%
both amongst my
colleagues and students.

00:20:26.291 --> 00:20:27.559
- Yeah, even though

00:20:27.559 --> 00:20:29.061
I'll let you save that
story for your memoir,

00:20:29.061 --> 00:20:30.329
but do you find

00:20:30.329 --> 00:20:33.332
that there is some sort
of spiritual awakening?

00:20:33.332 --> 00:20:37.803 align:start position:0%
I've sort of sensed that
because the progressive movement

00:20:37.803 --> 00:20:40.472
has just become this
secularism on steroids,

00:20:40.472 --> 00:20:42.874
government everything monster,

00:20:42.874 --> 00:20:44.309
that an awful lot of people,

00:20:44.309 --> 00:20:45.544 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I would include
myself in this,

00:20:45.544 --> 00:20:47.746 align:start size:88% position:13%
who are not
traditionally religious,

00:20:47.746 --> 00:20:49.948
are finding answers
in different ways.

00:20:51.083 --> 00:20:52.451
- Well, I hope that's right.

00:20:53.485 --> 00:20:54.853
I just gave you my report

00:20:54.853 --> 00:20:56.388
about what's going
on here at Brown.

00:20:56.388 --> 00:20:59.891
I can't speak beyond that,
but I hope that's right.

00:20:59.891 --> 00:21:01.627
- Yeah, interesting.

00:21:01.627 --> 00:21:03.395
All right, so let's move on
to some of the other things.

00:21:03.395 --> 00:21:04.830
Well, first off,

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:05.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
according to my paperwork
here, you're black.

00:21:05.931 --> 00:21:07.532
Is that, is that...

00:21:07.532 --> 00:21:09.635
- (chuckles) Last time
I checked I'm black.

00:21:09.635 --> 00:21:12.738
I was born in Chicago,
1948, on the South Side.

00:21:12.738 --> 00:21:15.641 align:start size:94% position:6%
My mother and father were
also born in Chicago.

00:21:15.641 --> 00:21:17.009
They were black. I'm black.

00:21:17.009 --> 00:21:18.944
- Okay, so as a black man

00:21:18.944 --> 00:21:20.779
that's a professor at
a liberal university,

00:21:20.779 --> 00:21:25.617
I unfortunately end up having
to ask all of my black guests,

00:21:25.617 --> 00:21:27.719 align:start size:97% position:3%
or I would rather say guests
who happen to be black

00:21:27.719 --> 00:21:28.954
that are doing something

00:21:28.954 --> 00:21:30.389
sort of similar to
what you are doing,

00:21:30.389 --> 00:21:31.657
can you just talk a little bit

00:21:31.657 --> 00:21:33.325
about the type of
treatment you get?

00:21:33.325 --> 00:21:34.793
Not from some of
the good students,

00:21:34.793 --> 00:21:38.930 align:start size:94% position:6%
but just sort of from the,
say, antiracist crowd,

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:41.033 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause they're not
that thrilled with you.

00:21:42.968 --> 00:21:46.438 align:start size:97% position:3%
- (sighs) You know, I'm
probably the wrong guy to ask.

00:21:46.438 --> 00:21:49.308
I mean, I'm 72 years old, okay?

00:21:49.308 --> 00:21:51.510 align:start size:84% position:16%
I know it doesn't
show, but it's true.

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:53.412
Born in 1948,

00:21:53.412 --> 00:21:57.783 align:start size:84% position:16%
so I'm a senior member
of the community.

00:21:57.783 --> 00:22:00.452
I've been around for a long
time. I'm a known quantity.

00:22:02.287 --> 00:22:03.989 align:start size:84% position:16%
I have the various
accolades that I have.

00:22:03.989 --> 00:22:07.526 align:start position:0%
I've had a distinguished
career as a research economist.

00:22:07.526 --> 00:22:12.264 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't think people really
tell me what they think

00:22:12.264 --> 00:22:13.865 line:0%
or react to me...

00:22:14.966 --> 00:22:17.569 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
It may be certain
deference to my position

00:22:17.569 --> 00:22:20.439 line:0%
and to my senior status.

00:22:20.439 --> 00:22:24.676
It may be out of respect for
my academic accomplishments.

00:22:24.676 --> 00:22:26.912
I hold a chair here
at Brown, et cetera.

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:29.715
I think they may whisper

00:22:30.749 --> 00:22:33.352
out of my earshot this or that,

00:22:33.352 --> 00:22:35.487
but I don't feel the chill.

00:22:35.487 --> 00:22:38.890
Now, COVID, COVID has
had an impact here

00:22:38.890 --> 00:22:41.126 align:start size:84% position:16%
because we're not
coming face to face.

00:22:41.126 --> 00:22:44.296 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're meeting through
Zoom, and Skype, and such,

00:22:44.296 --> 00:22:46.465 align:start size:81% position:19%
and this last year
has been a year

00:22:46.465 --> 00:22:50.335 align:start size:88% position:13%
in which some of my
more conservative ideas,

00:22:50.335 --> 00:22:53.171
especially about race,
and diversity, and equity

00:22:53.171 --> 00:22:56.108
have come to a head because
of the reckoning about race

00:22:56.108 --> 00:22:58.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the aftermath of George
Floyd, and all of that.

00:22:58.877 --> 00:23:02.080
I've written pieces that have
gotten a lot of positive play

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:04.483
in conservative quarters,

00:23:04.483 --> 00:23:07.252
which I'm sure some of
my colleagues despise,

00:23:07.252 --> 00:23:08.487
but I'm not seeing them

00:23:08.487 --> 00:23:10.489
around the lunch table
at the faculty club,

00:23:10.489 --> 00:23:13.692
so that they can convey
their contempt for me

00:23:13.692 --> 00:23:15.894
with their glare
or their whatever,

00:23:15.894 --> 00:23:19.097 align:start size:84% position:16%
so I don't know
about the last year,

00:23:19.097 --> 00:23:20.599
but before that,

00:23:20.599 --> 00:23:22.534
I would say it was kind of
like a gentleman's agreement

00:23:22.534 --> 00:23:25.971
that Glenn is a curmudgeon.

00:23:25.971 --> 00:23:29.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
Glenn is Glenn. Yeah, that's
where he's coming from.

00:23:29.674 --> 00:23:30.942
He's not a bad guy.

00:23:30.942 --> 00:23:32.844
He's all screwed up
about this stuff,

00:23:32.844 --> 00:23:34.045
but that's all right.

00:23:34.045 --> 00:23:35.247 align:start size:81% position:19%
We'll still invite
him to dinner,

00:23:35.247 --> 00:23:36.515
and I don't know
what's gonna happen

00:23:36.515 --> 00:23:39.017
once we get back to
business as usual.

00:23:39.017 --> 00:23:40.218
We'll see.

00:23:40.218 --> 00:23:41.620
- It's kind of funny
idea for a short story:

00:23:41.620 --> 00:23:43.121
a conservative professor

00:23:43.121 --> 00:23:45.056
who, because of lockdowns,
can feel more emboldened

00:23:45.056 --> 00:23:47.225
because the colleagues
aren't gonna sneer at him

00:23:47.225 --> 00:23:49.795
at the commissary or
something, you know?

00:23:49.795 --> 00:23:51.296
- Somebody ought to be
working on that story.

00:23:51.296 --> 00:23:52.697
- (chuckles) Somebody should be.

00:23:52.697 --> 00:23:54.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
Do you sense there's
anything left?

00:23:54.833 --> 00:23:56.768 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I get you're
not a Democrat.

00:23:56.768 --> 00:23:58.103
I haven't heard you say
you're a Republican,

00:23:58.103 --> 00:23:59.871
but I get you're not a Democrat.

00:23:59.871 --> 00:24:01.339
- I'm a registered independent

00:24:01.339 --> 00:24:03.475
who's playing both sides
of the street, so to speak,

00:24:03.475 --> 00:24:04.576
but I'm not very happy

00:24:04.576 --> 00:24:05.777 align:start size:88% position:13%
with where the
Democrats are right now.

00:24:05.777 --> 00:24:06.745
- Yeah, I mean, do you sense,

00:24:06.745 --> 00:24:08.246
is there anything left there

00:24:08.246 --> 00:24:11.183
that is in line with the
ideas that you believe in,

00:24:11.183 --> 00:24:12.417
that you think is in line

00:24:12.417 --> 00:24:13.885
with the ideas of
Martin Luther King?

00:24:13.885 --> 00:24:15.353
- The Democrats are nothing

00:24:15.353 --> 00:24:19.090 align:start size:97% position:3%
without the black voting 95%,
90% for their candidates.

00:24:19.090 --> 00:24:23.195
They would lose control of
everything without this.

00:24:23.195 --> 00:24:24.696
Biden wouldn't be
president today

00:24:24.696 --> 00:24:27.632
if James Clyburn were not able
to corral enough black votes

00:24:27.632 --> 00:24:30.035
at South Carolina's
Democratic Primary

00:24:30.035 --> 00:24:31.536
to get him out of the hole,

00:24:32.771 --> 00:24:36.475
so here's what I see.

00:24:36.475 --> 00:24:38.376
I'm not a professional
political scientist,

00:24:38.376 --> 00:24:40.946
so you can take it
for what it's worth.

00:24:40.946 --> 00:24:42.581
They're buying black votes

00:24:42.581 --> 00:24:45.050
by waving a bloody
shirt of racism.

00:24:45.050 --> 00:24:46.151
That's what they're doing.

00:24:46.151 --> 00:24:48.119
They're selling this idea

00:24:48.119 --> 00:24:50.489
that George Floyd's
death was yet one

00:24:50.489 --> 00:24:52.691 align:start size:84% position:16%
of another, of
another, of another

00:24:52.691 --> 00:24:56.061 align:start size:84% position:16%
racist state killing
of black people.

00:24:56.061 --> 00:24:57.562
There's "Open Season,"

00:24:57.562 --> 00:24:59.931
as Ben Crump says in the title
of his book on black people.

00:24:59.931 --> 00:25:03.735
Now, black people, be afraid,
be afraid, be very afraid.

00:25:03.735 --> 00:25:05.704
White racists are
coming to get you.

00:25:05.704 --> 00:25:07.706 align:start size:81% position:19%
They're in your
police department.

00:25:07.706 --> 00:25:08.940
They're right around the corner,

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:11.076
and we are the only
thing between you

00:25:11.076 --> 00:25:12.711
and a noose around your neck.

00:25:12.711 --> 00:25:13.879
That's what they're saying.

00:25:13.879 --> 00:25:15.380
It's not the new Jim...

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:18.617 align:start size:97% position:3%
The Georgia voting integrity
law is not just Jim Crow.

00:25:18.617 --> 00:25:22.153 align:start size:97% position:3%
"It's Jim Eagle. They wanna
put you all in chains."

00:25:22.153 --> 00:25:24.823 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's a quote from
President Joseph Biden:

00:25:24.823 --> 00:25:27.826 align:start size:84% position:16%
"They wanna put y'all
back at chains."

00:25:27.826 --> 00:25:31.997 align:start position:0%
Okay, so if black people, I
speak now to African Americans,

00:25:31.997 --> 00:25:35.567 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you happen to be watching
"The Dave Rubin Report,"

00:25:35.567 --> 00:25:37.402 align:start size:81% position:19%
if you fall for
this, we're fools.

00:25:37.402 --> 00:25:40.105 align:start size:84% position:16%
If we fall for
this, we are fools.

00:25:40.105 --> 00:25:44.009
We're being led around with
a ring through our noses,

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:50.715
so they show nothing but
contempt for black people.

00:25:51.883 --> 00:25:53.084 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm talking about
Joseph R. Biden now.

00:25:54.519 --> 00:25:55.654
- I'm with you.

00:25:55.654 --> 00:25:56.888
I know my audience is with you,

00:25:56.888 --> 00:25:58.990 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I think,
actually, truthfully,

00:25:58.990 --> 00:26:00.792 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think most of
America is with you.

00:26:00.792 --> 00:26:03.194 align:start size:88% position:13%
We just don't know where
the brave voices are.

00:26:03.194 --> 00:26:04.496 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that's part
of the problem.

00:26:04.496 --> 00:26:05.430
I mean, you're one of them,

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:07.232
but I think that we're living

00:26:07.232 --> 00:26:09.834 align:start size:81% position:19%
in this bravery
deficit right now.

00:26:09.834 --> 00:26:13.104 align:start position:0%
- These issues, the racial
conflict coming out of slavery,

00:26:13.104 --> 00:26:15.440 align:start size:84% position:16%
threaten the existence
of the Republic.

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:18.143
600,000 people in a
country of 30 million

00:26:18.143 --> 00:26:21.046 align:start size:97% position:3%
were slaughtered on the
battlefields of the Civil War,

00:26:21.046 --> 00:26:22.581
the consequence of which

00:26:22.581 --> 00:26:25.317
was the emancipation
of the African slaves.

00:26:25.317 --> 00:26:27.152
Here we are now,
150 years later,

00:26:27.152 --> 00:26:28.987 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the descendants
of those slaves

00:26:28.987 --> 00:26:31.056
are citizens of the
freest, richest,

00:26:31.056 --> 00:26:34.993
and most powerful republic
in the history of the world.

00:26:34.993 --> 00:26:36.528 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Man, Glenn, you wanna
run for president?

00:26:36.528 --> 00:26:38.597
I got it: you're
72, but come on.

00:26:38.597 --> 00:26:41.866
Come on, that's young for
a president these days.

00:26:41.866 --> 00:26:43.335
- Thanks. I appreciate that.

00:26:44.703 --> 00:26:45.971
That's not the burden

00:26:45.971 --> 00:26:47.405
I was thinking about
taking up right now.

00:26:47.405 --> 00:26:48.640
I was looking for the beach.

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:50.408
I was looking to put my
feet up now. (laughs)

00:26:50.408 --> 00:26:53.011
- Fair enough. Fair
enough. (chuckles)

00:26:53.011 --> 00:26:55.280
Well, can you paint a way,

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:57.082
or do you see a way out of this?

00:26:57.082 --> 00:26:58.516 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think a lot of
people sort of think

00:26:58.516 --> 00:27:01.653
we're just in this
endless descent.

00:27:01.653 --> 00:27:03.855
We can address the things
that you've addressed.

00:27:03.855 --> 00:27:05.290
We could say, "No,
you guys are wrong

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:07.425 align:start size:84% position:16%
"because we have to
talk about disparities

00:27:07.425 --> 00:27:08.693
"instead of discrimination.

00:27:08.693 --> 00:27:10.462
"We have to define
things properly,"

00:27:10.462 --> 00:27:11.730
but I think a lot
of people think,

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:15.600
"Oh, we just can't
stop this force."

00:27:17.602 --> 00:27:19.971
- I don't know how it stops.
I'm being honest with you.

00:27:19.971 --> 00:27:22.374 align:start size:84% position:16%
I feel like I'm
spitting in the wind.

00:27:22.374 --> 00:27:25.076 align:start size:88% position:13%
My little humble efforts
with John McWhorter,

00:27:25.076 --> 00:27:27.045
we have a podcast,
"The Glenn Show,"

00:27:27.045 --> 00:27:29.347 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we talk about these
things and whatnot.

00:27:29.347 --> 00:27:32.984 align:start position:0%
That's at patreon.com/glennshow,
two N's, one word.

00:27:34.052 --> 00:27:35.553
You know...

00:27:35.553 --> 00:27:36.721
- We'll link to it, but I
gotta get you on locals.

00:27:36.721 --> 00:27:37.956
That's a different topic.

00:27:37.956 --> 00:27:40.725
- Oh, okay, but
in any case, man,

00:27:40.725 --> 00:27:43.495
I think we had a chance,

00:27:43.495 --> 00:27:46.164
with the presidency
of Barack Obama,

00:27:47.799 --> 00:27:50.468 align:start size:84% position:16%
to really change the
conversation, and
that he let us down.

00:27:51.836 --> 00:27:53.705 align:start size:97% position:3%
We can talk about the
presidency of Barack Obama.

00:27:53.705 --> 00:27:54.973
It is many, many things.

00:27:54.973 --> 00:27:57.742 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's not just this,
but on this portfolio,

00:27:57.742 --> 00:27:59.678
"If I had a son, he'd
look like Trayvon."

00:27:59.678 --> 00:28:01.146
He shouldn't have said that.

00:28:01.146 --> 00:28:02.714 align:start size:88% position:13%
Shouldn't have said,
"My friend, Henry Gates,

00:28:02.714 --> 00:28:03.948
"the police acted stupidly."

00:28:03.948 --> 00:28:05.550
We could go through
all these things.

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:06.818
He shouldn't have
made Al Sharpton,

00:28:06.818 --> 00:28:09.120
200 times visiting
the White House,

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:12.190
ambassador to black America
from his administration.

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:13.525
He shouldn't have done that.

00:28:13.525 --> 00:28:14.959 align:start size:84% position:16%
He should have stood
for law and order

00:28:14.959 --> 00:28:16.428
when Baltimore was burning down,

00:28:16.428 --> 00:28:17.595 align:start size:88% position:13%
when Ferguson, Missouri
was burning down

00:28:17.595 --> 00:28:19.064 align:start size:84% position:16%
instead of splitting
the difference

00:28:19.064 --> 00:28:21.399
and saying they have
legitimate grievances.

00:28:21.399 --> 00:28:22.867 align:start size:84% position:16%
Of course they have
legitimate grievances,

00:28:22.867 --> 00:28:25.036 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it doesn't rise
to the level of arson,

00:28:25.036 --> 00:28:27.872
and assault on police
officers, and looting.

00:28:27.872 --> 00:28:31.076 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's unforgivable.
That cannot be tolerated.

00:28:31.076 --> 00:28:32.644 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't care what
their grievances are,

00:28:32.644 --> 00:28:34.446
and he should have said so.

00:28:34.446 --> 00:28:36.081 align:start size:84% position:16%
He should have turned
to black America,

00:28:36.081 --> 00:28:38.616
as he tried to do a little
bit during the campaign,

00:28:38.616 --> 00:28:40.952
but he beat a retreat from it,

00:28:40.952 --> 00:28:42.987
and said, "Yeah, there's
racism, but you know what?

00:28:42.987 --> 00:28:44.556
"We need to pull up our socks."

00:28:44.556 --> 00:28:46.391 align:start size:88% position:13%
He could have found a
better way of saying it.

00:28:46.391 --> 00:28:48.893 align:start size:97% position:3%
He could have found an
Obamaesque way of saying it.

00:28:48.893 --> 00:28:51.096 align:start size:97% position:3%
"We need to stand up straight
with our shoulders back,"

00:28:51.096 --> 00:28:53.098
as Jordan Peterson could say.

00:28:53.098 --> 00:28:55.800
We need to take care of our
kids. We need to get busy.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:57.602 align:start size:84% position:16%
Racism is not the
first, or the second,

00:28:57.602 --> 00:29:00.171 align:start size:81% position:19%
or the third issue
confronting us.

00:29:00.171 --> 00:29:01.706
He could have said that.

00:29:01.706 --> 00:29:03.875
He could have shut up
some of these people

00:29:03.875 --> 00:29:05.376
like Ta-Nehisi Coates.

00:29:05.376 --> 00:29:08.747
Instead, he invites them
for cozy talks, et cetera.

00:29:08.747 --> 00:29:10.682
Shut them up by saying,

00:29:10.682 --> 00:29:15.086 align:start size:94% position:6%
"My occupying this
office, commander in chief

00:29:15.086 --> 00:29:17.822
"of the most powerful
military on the planet,

00:29:17.822 --> 00:29:20.225 align:start size:84% position:16%
"the agenda-setter for
this great republic

00:29:20.225 --> 00:29:23.128
"of 325 million people,

00:29:23.128 --> 00:29:27.732 align:start size:97% position:3%
"gives the lie to your talk
about oppressive denial

00:29:27.732 --> 00:29:29.901
"of the integrity
of black bodies."

00:29:29.901 --> 00:29:32.403
He said it in 2004,
Barack Obama did.

00:29:32.403 --> 00:29:34.305
He said, "It's in my very DNA."

00:29:34.305 --> 00:29:35.774
He's black and he's white.

00:29:35.774 --> 00:29:38.376 align:start size:88% position:13%
We all are,
metaphorically speaking.

00:29:38.376 --> 00:29:42.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's only one people
here: the American people.

00:29:42.147 --> 00:29:44.349
There's only one people here.

00:29:45.784 --> 00:29:49.087
For a variety of reasons,
President Obama elected,

00:29:49.087 --> 00:29:50.355
and it's not an easy job.

00:29:50.355 --> 00:29:51.923
It's a hard job. That's
why I don't want it.

00:29:51.923 --> 00:29:52.991
Thanks, anyway, Dave.

00:29:52.991 --> 00:29:55.293 align:start size:88% position:13%
- (laughs) I
apologize. I apologize.

00:29:55.293 --> 00:29:56.561
- For a variety of reasons,

00:29:56.561 --> 00:29:59.030
he elected not to
take that one on,

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:02.267
and he's the only person
who could have taken it on.

00:30:02.267 --> 00:30:03.468
He's the only person

00:30:03.468 --> 00:30:05.870
who could have sat
in the White House...

00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:07.338
You saw what happened

00:30:07.338 --> 00:30:08.907
when Trump tried to order
out the National Guard

00:30:08.907 --> 00:30:10.842
or whatever it was he
tried to do to deal,

00:30:10.842 --> 00:30:12.310
and everybody goes ballistic,

00:30:12.310 --> 00:30:14.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's all racist,
and what not.

00:30:14.078 --> 00:30:15.346
Obama's the only person

00:30:15.346 --> 00:30:17.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
who could have changed
that conversation

00:30:17.382 --> 00:30:20.151
by putting his racial identity

00:30:20.151 --> 00:30:22.554 align:start size:97% position:3%
and his civic
responsibility on the line,

00:30:22.554 --> 00:30:26.858
not as a fealty to the
black victim narrative,

00:30:26.858 --> 00:30:29.994
but as an affirmation of the
possibilities of the country.

00:30:29.994 --> 00:30:32.964
Now, it's easy for me
to say this, I know,

00:30:32.964 --> 00:30:36.134
but that's what I see when
I look at it in retrospect.

00:30:36.134 --> 00:30:37.635
An opportunity was missed

00:30:37.635 --> 00:30:42.073
between 2009 and 2017,

00:30:42.073 --> 00:30:44.342
and where we are now,

00:30:44.342 --> 00:30:47.879 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, I heard Michelle
Obama, the former First Lady,

00:30:47.879 --> 00:30:52.183
say to Gayle King at "CBS
Morning News" the other day

00:30:52.183 --> 00:30:54.819
that she worried that her
daughters Sasha and Malia

00:30:54.819 --> 00:30:57.555
might be set upon by
rogue police officers

00:30:57.555 --> 00:30:58.790
because they're black,

00:30:58.790 --> 00:31:00.658 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they might be
mistaken for somebody,

00:31:00.658 --> 00:31:02.594
and to that, I have to say this,

00:31:02.594 --> 00:31:05.029 align:start position:0%
I mean, I said this on my
podcast, so I'll say it to you.

00:31:05.029 --> 00:31:07.465
First of all, it's
false. It's false.

00:31:07.465 --> 00:31:10.301
You gotta get past the Secret
Service to get to those kids.

00:31:10.301 --> 00:31:11.569
She knows it's false.

00:31:11.569 --> 00:31:15.139
Secondly, she knows that
what she said is untrue,

00:31:15.139 --> 00:31:16.541
So what is she doing?

00:31:16.541 --> 00:31:19.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
She's playing you Negroes.
She's playing you.

00:31:19.377 --> 00:31:22.747
She's enacting a trope,

00:31:22.747 --> 00:31:27.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
a narrative in the service
of a political program,

00:31:27.151 --> 00:31:30.188
which is to keep you
on the reservation,

00:31:30.188 --> 00:31:32.323
afraid that the boogeyman racist

00:31:32.323 --> 00:31:33.858
is gonna come and
get your children.

00:31:33.858 --> 00:31:36.261
She's not worried about her
children being harassed by,

00:31:36.261 --> 00:31:37.695
on Martha's Vineyard,

00:31:37.695 --> 00:31:41.532
by a cop because they're black,

00:31:41.532 --> 00:31:44.669
so instead of pushing

00:31:44.669 --> 00:31:47.906
against this narrative,
which is comforting.

00:31:47.906 --> 00:31:50.775
It is a comfortable place
to be if you're black:

00:31:50.775 --> 00:31:52.076
I'm a victim.

00:31:52.076 --> 00:31:53.278
They wouldn't let me
get the Nobel Prize

00:31:53.278 --> 00:31:54.579
because I was black.

00:31:54.579 --> 00:31:55.880
I'm an economist. I'm
a damn good economist.

00:31:55.880 --> 00:31:58.483
I want the Nobel prize.
I want the Nobel Prize!

00:31:58.483 --> 00:32:01.786
They wouldn't let me have
it 'cause I'm black, okay?

00:32:01.786 --> 00:32:05.623 align:start size:88% position:13%
That is getting old,
but we need our leaders,

00:32:05.623 --> 00:32:10.028 align:start position:0%
I'm talking about African
American leaders, to point away,

00:32:10.028 --> 00:32:13.564 align:start size:94% position:6%
point away from that,
and they're not doing it.

00:32:13.564 --> 00:32:15.566
- So let's shift
a little bit back

00:32:15.566 --> 00:32:17.368
to the academic part of this

00:32:17.368 --> 00:32:19.337
because related to all of this

00:32:19.337 --> 00:32:21.439
is the fact that
academic standards

00:32:21.439 --> 00:32:23.808
are basically being
obliterated right now

00:32:23.808 --> 00:32:25.743
in the name of equity,

00:32:25.743 --> 00:32:27.478
in terms of testing,

00:32:27.478 --> 00:32:30.014 align:start size:97% position:3%
in terms of, you know
what's happening at Harvard,

00:32:30.014 --> 00:32:32.216
where they're actually
punishing Asian students

00:32:32.216 --> 00:32:33.618
at the admissions level.

00:32:33.618 --> 00:32:36.955 align:start size:84% position:16%
That must, putting
aside the racial part,

00:32:36.955 --> 00:32:38.323
just as an academic,

00:32:38.323 --> 00:32:40.224
as someone at a place
of higher education,

00:32:40.224 --> 00:32:41.793
that must drive you crazy.

00:32:43.528 --> 00:32:44.963
- Well, I'm against it.

00:32:44.963 --> 00:32:47.732
I signed on to the amicus brief

00:32:47.732 --> 00:32:50.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
that some economists
put together

00:32:50.201 --> 00:32:52.804 align:start size:97% position:3%
to support the Asians in
their lawsuit against Harvard

00:32:52.804 --> 00:32:55.239
because I disagree
with that policy,

00:32:56.841 --> 00:32:59.410 align:start size:84% position:16%
but many of us
here in the academy

00:32:59.410 --> 00:33:03.214 align:start size:81% position:19%
are full score
behind this stuff.

00:33:05.283 --> 00:33:08.086 align:start size:84% position:16%
It breaks my heart,
to be honest with you,

00:33:08.086 --> 00:33:11.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it's an
invitation to mediocrity,

00:33:11.089 --> 00:33:15.493
and it's a kind of bluff

00:33:15.493 --> 00:33:16.961
and a shell game.

00:33:16.961 --> 00:33:19.364
Here, let me just be clear
about what I'm talking about.

00:33:19.364 --> 00:33:23.901
At the very most exclusive
venues of intellectual labor,

00:33:23.901 --> 00:33:27.005 align:start position:0%
like at a Princeton University,
or a Brown University,

00:33:27.005 --> 00:33:29.974
or a MIT, or a Caltech,

00:33:29.974 --> 00:33:33.077
the stuff is really hard, and
not everybody can do it well.

00:33:35.213 --> 00:33:36.014
It's hard.

00:33:36.014 --> 00:33:38.282 line:0%
It's hard reading Plato

00:33:38.282 --> 00:33:40.585 line:0%
and figuring out what
he was talking about.

00:33:40.585 --> 00:33:42.487 line:0%
It's hard doing physics.

00:33:42.487 --> 00:33:45.556
It's hard doing advanced
mathematics, and chemistry,

00:33:45.556 --> 00:33:46.858
and other STEM stuff like that.

00:33:46.858 --> 00:33:48.126
That stuff is difficult.

00:33:48.126 --> 00:33:50.461
Med school is hard.
Law school is hard.

00:33:50.461 --> 00:33:54.732 align:start size:84% position:16%
It requires real
intellectual mastery

00:33:54.732 --> 00:33:56.968
in order to be done effectively.

00:33:57.869 --> 00:34:00.304
Now, unfortunately,

00:34:00.304 --> 00:34:02.607
a proportionate number
of African Americans

00:34:02.607 --> 00:34:04.275
have not achieved that mastery.

00:34:05.410 --> 00:34:06.878
Now, we can go into
the reasons why.

00:34:06.878 --> 00:34:09.680
History has not been entirely
kind to African Americans.

00:34:09.680 --> 00:34:12.717
There's blame enough
to go around. Why?

00:34:12.717 --> 00:34:14.218
But the fact of the matter is

00:34:15.586 --> 00:34:18.656 align:start position:0%
a proportionate number, relative
to population, of blacks,

00:34:18.656 --> 00:34:20.058
so we're fewer

00:34:20.058 --> 00:34:23.327 align:start position:0%
in the venues where the
intellectual work is difficult.

00:34:23.327 --> 00:34:26.631
Now, there are two things you
can do in the face of that.

00:34:26.631 --> 00:34:28.800
One of them is to
lower the standards

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:30.034
so that you can increase

00:34:30.034 --> 00:34:31.669
the representation
of African Americans

00:34:31.669 --> 00:34:33.271
and call that inclusion.

00:34:34.705 --> 00:34:39.010
The other is to grasp the
nettle, bite the bullet,

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:40.344
whatever metaphor you want,

00:34:40.344 --> 00:34:44.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
face up to the
developmental deficiencies

00:34:44.315 --> 00:34:45.850
and address them,

00:34:45.850 --> 00:34:49.353 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I mean address them
from infancy, okay?

00:34:49.353 --> 00:34:51.155
Now, this is not laissez-faire.

00:34:51.155 --> 00:34:53.024
I'm not saying there could
be no government program.

00:34:53.024 --> 00:34:54.992
I'm not saying don't
have any education.

00:34:54.992 --> 00:34:56.527
Don't have any Head Start.

00:34:56.527 --> 00:34:59.430
Don't have any summer
program, whatever.

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:02.467 align:start position:0%
We can talk about what the
things are that need to be done,

00:35:02.467 --> 00:35:05.870 align:start size:97% position:3%
but can we first understand
what the problem is?

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:08.139
If our kids are testing poorly,

00:35:09.474 --> 00:35:13.811
it's because they don't
know the material, okay?

00:35:13.811 --> 00:35:16.414 align:start size:81% position:19%
If an Asian kid
from a poor family

00:35:16.414 --> 00:35:20.051 align:start size:97% position:3%
living in a two-room
apartment with four siblings

00:35:20.051 --> 00:35:23.054
can ace the test, our
kids can do it too.

00:35:24.222 --> 00:35:27.258
Anybody who doesn't
think so is a racist.

00:35:28.426 --> 00:35:29.660
They have the racism

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:32.897 align:start size:88% position:13%
of the bigotry of low
expectation about black.

00:35:32.897 --> 00:35:37.802
You write us off? You think
we're defeated by history?

00:35:37.802 --> 00:35:42.473
You think we're infants that
we can't actually perform?

00:35:42.473 --> 00:35:45.276
We can perform. We
need to do the work.

00:35:45.276 --> 00:35:48.446
Give us an opportunity.
Confront the deficits.

00:35:48.446 --> 00:35:51.382
Redress them. Keep the
level of playing field.

00:35:51.382 --> 00:35:53.017
Don't lower the bar for us,

00:35:53.017 --> 00:35:54.852
and we'll measure up in
the fullness of time.

00:35:54.852 --> 00:35:56.354
It may not happen tomorrow,

00:35:56.354 --> 00:35:57.722 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it may not
happen the next day,

00:35:57.722 --> 00:35:59.524
but it'll happen in
the fullness of time.

00:35:59.524 --> 00:36:02.160
I say this as a matter of faith,

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:05.229
so I think we make
the wrong choice.

00:36:05.229 --> 00:36:07.465
Now, I can understand in 1970,

00:36:07.465 --> 00:36:10.067 align:start size:97% position:3%
with all the
rabble-rousing and whatnot,

00:36:10.067 --> 00:36:11.269
and the universities

00:36:11.269 --> 00:36:15.139
had to meet the protest
halfway and whatnot,

00:36:15.139 --> 00:36:16.674
and they did what they did,

00:36:16.674 --> 00:36:19.043
but we're now in the year 2021.

00:36:19.043 --> 00:36:21.612
We're a half century past that,

00:36:21.612 --> 00:36:23.814
and we're laying
down a predicate

00:36:23.814 --> 00:36:25.283 align:start size:81% position:19%
for how it is that
we go forward,

00:36:25.283 --> 00:36:30.188 align:start size:97% position:3%
and this, "No, it's not
equality; it's equity" bunk

00:36:31.622 --> 00:36:35.626
is a surrender in the face
of the problem that we face.

00:36:35.626 --> 00:36:37.929 align:start size:84% position:16%
The problem is
develop black people

00:36:37.929 --> 00:36:42.667 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that proportionately more
of us exhibit the mastery

00:36:42.667 --> 00:36:46.204
requisite to being successful

00:36:46.204 --> 00:36:47.805
in these competitive venues.

00:36:47.805 --> 00:36:49.307
Did you see what happened?

00:36:49.307 --> 00:36:50.975
I rambled a bit, but thank
you for giving me the time.

00:36:50.975 --> 00:36:52.643
You see what happened at
the Georgetown Law Center?

00:36:52.643 --> 00:36:54.378
The Georgetown Law Center,

00:36:54.378 --> 00:36:57.648
this brouhaha from
a few months ago.

00:36:57.648 --> 00:36:59.517
A instructor, into a hot mic,

00:36:59.517 --> 00:37:01.519
not knowing that she
was being recorded,

00:37:01.519 --> 00:37:04.689
allowed as how the poor
performance in her class

00:37:04.689 --> 00:37:07.658
clustering at the bottom were
very disproportionately black.

00:37:07.658 --> 00:37:09.093
She said it upset her.

00:37:09.093 --> 00:37:10.394 align:start size:84% position:16%
She didn't know
what to do about it,

00:37:10.394 --> 00:37:11.963
but the black kids
were not doing well.

00:37:11.963 --> 00:37:13.164
Do you know what happened

00:37:13.164 --> 00:37:15.433
when it was revealed
that she had said this?

00:37:15.433 --> 00:37:19.403
They defined Georgetown Law
as a racist institution.

00:37:19.403 --> 00:37:22.273 align:start size:97% position:3%
The students demanded, and the
dean acceded to the demand,

00:37:22.273 --> 00:37:24.375
that this professor be fired.

00:37:24.375 --> 00:37:26.811
White professors
were called upon

00:37:26.811 --> 00:37:29.213
to get together and
issue a statement

00:37:29.213 --> 00:37:32.116 align:start size:81% position:19%
on behalf of their
white privilege

00:37:32.116 --> 00:37:34.385 align:start size:88% position:13%
taking responsibility
for the poor performance

00:37:34.385 --> 00:37:36.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the black students
in the law class.

00:37:36.320 --> 00:37:37.855
Now, here's what I know:

00:37:37.855 --> 00:37:42.193
contracts, constitutional law,

00:37:42.193 --> 00:37:44.695
torts, civil procedure,

00:37:44.695 --> 00:37:46.264 align:start size:84% position:16%
this is what you
study in law school,

00:37:46.264 --> 00:37:48.065
that stuff is hard, man.

00:37:48.065 --> 00:37:50.301 align:start size:84% position:16%
That stuff will
make your head hurt.

00:37:50.301 --> 00:37:52.503
Those big casebooks that you
see people carrying around

00:37:52.503 --> 00:37:53.671
and stuff like that...

00:37:53.671 --> 00:37:56.841 align:start size:84% position:16%
Writing an effective
legal argument

00:37:56.841 --> 00:37:58.709
is a real skill.

00:38:00.111 --> 00:38:01.846
What the professor was reporting

00:38:01.846 --> 00:38:04.482 align:start position:0%
is that the black kids at
Georgetown, this is Georgetown,

00:38:04.482 --> 00:38:08.319
this is one of the elite
law schools on the planet,

00:38:08.319 --> 00:38:11.756 align:start size:84% position:16%
disproportionately
don't have that skill.

00:38:11.756 --> 00:38:14.625
Now, you would have thought
that the administration,

00:38:14.625 --> 00:38:17.295 align:start size:97% position:3%
being informed of that,
would slap their foreheads,

00:38:17.295 --> 00:38:19.297
and says, "Oh my God,
let's do something

00:38:19.297 --> 00:38:21.966 align:start size:97% position:3%
"to make sure that our
youngsters who are of color

00:38:21.966 --> 00:38:23.434
"have the skill."

00:38:23.434 --> 00:38:27.672 align:start position:0%
No, no, they retreat behind
this institutional racism stuff,

00:38:27.672 --> 00:38:30.074
and it's patronizing

00:38:30.074 --> 00:38:32.843
and condescending
to blacks to do it

00:38:32.843 --> 00:38:34.312
because what you're
really saying is,

00:38:34.312 --> 00:38:36.080
"We don't think you're ever
gonna be able to cut it.

00:38:36.080 --> 00:38:38.316
"We'll cover for
you. It's okay."

00:38:38.316 --> 00:38:41.852
- So as a numbers guy,
and as an economist,

00:38:41.852 --> 00:38:43.688
have you ever seen an instance

00:38:43.688 --> 00:38:46.557 align:start size:84% position:16%
where equity actually
raises anyone?

00:38:46.557 --> 00:38:50.761
Or is it literally always
that the way we get equity

00:38:50.761 --> 00:38:52.897
is that you're bringing
down a certain class?

00:38:52.897 --> 00:38:55.132
Does it ever do
this other thing?

00:38:56.701 --> 00:39:01.172
- (sighs) Well, I'm not
sure how to answer that.

00:39:01.172 --> 00:39:05.543 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm sure I could
find examples where,

00:39:07.478 --> 00:39:09.013
if I did a little bit

00:39:09.013 --> 00:39:13.017
of leveling and
evening early on,

00:39:14.185 --> 00:39:16.887
the opportunities that
I generated for kids

00:39:16.887 --> 00:39:19.557
would give them a chance
to show what they can do,

00:39:19.557 --> 00:39:21.325
and they might not otherwise
be able to get in or whatever,

00:39:21.325 --> 00:39:22.793
but I'm hard-pressed.

00:39:22.793 --> 00:39:24.261 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm hard-pressed to actually
come up with an example,

00:39:24.261 --> 00:39:26.497
so I'll just answer no.

00:39:26.497 --> 00:39:29.033 align:start size:97% position:3%
- (chuckles) You definitely
can't be president

00:39:29.033 --> 00:39:31.102 align:start size:88% position:13%
because you forthrightly
answered a question

00:39:31.102 --> 00:39:33.337
that you weren't totally
sure of the answer on.

00:39:33.337 --> 00:39:35.172
(Glenn laughs)

00:39:35.172 --> 00:39:36.874 align:start size:84% position:16%
You mentioned
reparations before,

00:39:36.874 --> 00:39:39.543
and about, maybe about
four months ago or so,

00:39:39.543 --> 00:39:41.312 align:start size:84% position:16%
suddenly it was a
very hot conversation.

00:39:41.312 --> 00:39:43.314
Now there's a whole bunch
of other stuff going on,

00:39:43.314 --> 00:39:44.982 align:start size:97% position:3%
so it seems to have
tempered down a little bit,

00:39:44.982 --> 00:39:48.552 align:start size:97% position:3%
but we know it's gonna come
back into the equation.

00:39:48.552 --> 00:39:51.756 align:start size:97% position:3%
You're against reparations.
Can you explain why?

00:39:51.756 --> 00:39:53.557
I mean, I think it's
a clear through line

00:39:53.557 --> 00:39:54.525
to everything we've discussed,

00:39:54.525 --> 00:39:55.993
but I wanna let you answer it-

00:39:55.993 --> 00:39:57.928 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, it's certainly related
to what I've been saying,

00:39:57.928 --> 00:40:00.531
but don't do that
to our country.

00:40:00.531 --> 00:40:02.066
Don't do that to our country.

00:40:02.066 --> 00:40:04.769
Here, so let's be specific.

00:40:04.769 --> 00:40:06.337
You know about the Japanese

00:40:06.337 --> 00:40:08.973
who were interned by the
Roosevelt administration

00:40:08.973 --> 00:40:10.374
in the Second World War,

00:40:10.374 --> 00:40:11.976 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they lost their property,
and their businesses,

00:40:11.976 --> 00:40:14.111 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it was a horrible thing,
their civil liberties.

00:40:14.111 --> 00:40:15.446 align:start size:84% position:16%
These were citizens,
some of them,

00:40:15.446 --> 00:40:17.415
many of them, most of them,

00:40:17.415 --> 00:40:20.584
so Ronald Reagan
signed into law,

00:40:20.584 --> 00:40:22.420
and I think George
Herbert Walker Bush

00:40:22.420 --> 00:40:24.321
presided over the administration

00:40:24.321 --> 00:40:27.191 align:start size:84% position:16%
of a statute that
recognized the harm,

00:40:27.191 --> 00:40:30.428
apologized to those people,
and paid them reparations.

00:40:30.428 --> 00:40:34.932
They got 20,000 a head.
There was 80,000 of them.

00:40:34.932 --> 00:40:38.769
80,000 people, $20,000 a
head, that's $1.6 billion,

00:40:39.770 --> 00:40:41.205
and the people who got paid

00:40:41.205 --> 00:40:43.040
were the ones who
got dispossessed,

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:44.875
not their children, not
their grandchildren,

00:40:44.875 --> 00:40:45.943
not their cousins.

00:40:45.943 --> 00:40:47.912
- [Dave] Right.

00:40:47.912 --> 00:40:51.582
- 20,000 a head, 80,000
people, $1.6 billion,

00:40:53.017 --> 00:40:55.386
so we could do that, and
we should have done it.

00:40:56.987 --> 00:40:58.389
Now, you take African Americans.

00:40:58.389 --> 00:40:59.657
We're 40 million.

00:41:01.592 --> 00:41:03.461
I don't know what
40 acres and a mule

00:41:03.461 --> 00:41:04.962
comes to in the current day,

00:41:04.962 --> 00:41:07.298
but they tell me it's in the
tens of thousands of dollars,

00:41:07.298 --> 00:41:11.001
so let's just say $100,000 to
be on the (indistinct), okay?

00:41:11.001 --> 00:41:13.270
That's $4 trillion

00:41:15.439 --> 00:41:16.540
with a T.

00:41:18.876 --> 00:41:23.848 align:start size:84% position:16%
The interned Japanese
got 1.6 billion,

00:41:23.848 --> 00:41:25.850
80,000 of them.

00:41:25.850 --> 00:41:28.886
These people are talking
about trillions of dollars

00:41:28.886 --> 00:41:30.287
being, in one way or another,

00:41:30.287 --> 00:41:33.557 align:start size:88% position:13%
transferred to
Americans who are black.

00:41:33.557 --> 00:41:36.827
That's a Social Security-scale

00:41:36.827 --> 00:41:40.030
enactment of public distribution

00:41:40.030 --> 00:41:41.999
based on the race
of the citizen.

00:41:43.834 --> 00:41:45.469
That's South Africaesque.

00:41:45.469 --> 00:41:47.271
We don't want that
for our country.

00:41:47.271 --> 00:41:49.673 align:start position:0%
I don't say that as a black
man. I say that as an American.

00:41:49.673 --> 00:41:50.975
We don't want that
for our country.

00:41:50.975 --> 00:41:53.177
That's not the way we do this.

00:41:53.177 --> 00:41:56.580
Now, if we're concerned about
people who are disadvantaged,

00:41:56.580 --> 00:41:58.582
we can direct ourselves
to their disadvantage,

00:41:58.582 --> 00:42:01.585
and by the way, I don't
really see an argument

00:42:01.585 --> 00:42:03.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
for overlooking a
disadvantaged white person

00:42:03.854 --> 00:42:06.423 align:start size:97% position:3%
on my way to helping a
disadvantaged black person,

00:42:06.423 --> 00:42:09.293 align:start size:97% position:3%
so what I think the advocates
of reparations should do,

00:42:09.293 --> 00:42:12.096 align:start position:0%
easy for me to say, they're
not listening to me for advice,

00:42:12.096 --> 00:42:14.698 align:start size:97% position:3%
is if they want to see more
money spent on schools,

00:42:14.698 --> 00:42:16.634
if they wanna see more
money spent on welfare,

00:42:16.634 --> 00:42:18.836
if they wanna see more
money spent on education,

00:42:18.836 --> 00:42:21.739 align:start size:88% position:13%
or whatever, on
housing, on healthcare,

00:42:22.806 --> 00:42:25.175
put the weight of their advocacy

00:42:25.175 --> 00:42:27.611 align:start position:0%
behind whatever the progressive
political program is

00:42:27.611 --> 00:42:29.547 align:start size:97% position:3%
that they think is gonna
deliver that for themselves,

00:42:29.547 --> 00:42:32.049
I might not support
every letter of it,

00:42:32.049 --> 00:42:33.517 align:start size:81% position:19%
but that's neither
here nor there.

00:42:33.517 --> 00:42:35.019
That's for the country,

00:42:35.019 --> 00:42:37.021
so that's one argument I
have against reparations.

00:42:37.021 --> 00:42:39.123 align:start size:84% position:16%
Please don't do
that to my country.

00:42:39.123 --> 00:42:40.824 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're already divided
enough by race,

00:42:40.824 --> 00:42:42.726
but there's another
argument that I have

00:42:42.726 --> 00:42:45.029 align:start size:81% position:19%
that I think is
just as important,

00:42:45.029 --> 00:42:49.700 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is the problems
besetting the black poor

00:42:49.700 --> 00:42:51.669
are not gonna go away

00:42:51.669 --> 00:42:55.573 align:start size:88% position:13%
when you enact some kind
of statute like this.

00:42:55.573 --> 00:42:57.241
I'm talking about the
out-of-wedlock births.

00:42:57.241 --> 00:42:58.776
I'm talking about the
single-parent families.

00:42:58.776 --> 00:43:01.478
I'm talking about the poor
reading and math skills.

00:43:01.478 --> 00:43:03.614 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
I'm talking about
the low work habits.

00:43:03.614 --> 00:43:04.882 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
I'm talking about
the number of people

00:43:04.882 --> 00:43:06.383 line:0%
involved in criminal activity,

00:43:06.383 --> 00:43:09.086 align:start position:0% line:0%
the people who are in the
prisons, and so forth and so on.

00:43:09.086 --> 00:43:11.989 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's gonna continue
to be an issue

00:43:11.989 --> 00:43:14.124 align:start size:84% position:16%
in Detroit,
Cleveland, St. Louis,

00:43:14.124 --> 00:43:16.694
South Side, West Side
of Chicago, et cetera,

00:43:16.694 --> 00:43:19.630
and many, many other places
besides in this country.

00:43:20.965 --> 00:43:23.834 align:start size:97% position:3%
If you sit down at a
bargaining table with America

00:43:23.834 --> 00:43:25.035
and cut a deal,

00:43:25.035 --> 00:43:27.371 align:start size:88% position:13%
you cut a side deal
with America for blacks

00:43:27.371 --> 00:43:28.973
on behalf of that,

00:43:28.973 --> 00:43:31.475
and you get paid,
whatever you get,

00:43:31.475 --> 00:43:34.578
that commodifies your
claim on the country

00:43:34.578 --> 00:43:36.513
and discharges the obligation.

00:43:36.513 --> 00:43:38.048
Now you're on your own.

00:43:38.048 --> 00:43:41.719
Next time around, people will
rightly be able to say to you,

00:43:41.719 --> 00:43:44.121 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Don't come to me
with your bellyaching

00:43:44.121 --> 00:43:45.289
"about this or about that.

00:43:45.289 --> 00:43:46.724
"You have been paid."

00:43:47.625 --> 00:43:48.792
Why would we do that?

00:43:48.792 --> 00:43:51.795 align:start size:88% position:13%
Just from a purely
tactical point of view,

00:43:51.795 --> 00:43:53.530
that's not a good play,

00:43:53.530 --> 00:43:55.566
so it's bad for the country,

00:43:55.566 --> 00:43:56.834
and at the end of the day,

00:43:56.834 --> 00:43:58.469
even though it may
not be apparent,

00:43:58.469 --> 00:44:00.838
it's also bad for black
people, in my opinion.

00:44:00.838 --> 00:44:02.106
- Can I toss in one more?

00:44:02.106 --> 00:44:03.540
I'd love to know what
you think about this,

00:44:03.540 --> 00:44:05.943 align:start size:94% position:6%
that it also sort of
ignores human psychology,

00:44:05.943 --> 00:44:08.379
which is that even if
you did the 4 trillion,

00:44:08.379 --> 00:44:10.347
and everyone gets
a hundred grand,

00:44:10.347 --> 00:44:13.117 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the progressives
of tomorrow

00:44:13.117 --> 00:44:15.486
will tell the people
who accept the money,

00:44:15.486 --> 00:44:17.855 align:start size:84% position:16%
"You've sold us
out for that money"

00:44:17.855 --> 00:44:19.757
because of what you
just laid out there,

00:44:19.757 --> 00:44:22.059
that most likely the
problems don't go away,

00:44:22.059 --> 00:44:25.329 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then suddenly the
progressives of 2040 will say,

00:44:25.329 --> 00:44:26.797
"Well, either we want more,

00:44:26.797 --> 00:44:28.966
"or the generation before us

00:44:28.966 --> 00:44:30.934
"was just part of the
evil, corrupt system,"

00:44:30.934 --> 00:44:32.870
or something like that.

00:44:32.870 --> 00:44:34.438
- Yeah, that's probably right,

00:44:34.438 --> 00:44:36.607
and it's probably the case

00:44:36.607 --> 00:44:39.810
that they will be able to
get something out of that

00:44:39.810 --> 00:44:44.348 align:start size:97% position:3%
if the politics of the
current day is still going on.

00:44:44.348 --> 00:44:46.450
I mean, not withstanding the
fact that you've been paid,

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:50.320
you'd still be able to milk
the system for something

00:44:50.320 --> 00:44:52.990 align:start size:81% position:19%
based on that kind
of an argument.

00:44:52.990 --> 00:44:54.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't know. I can't
think that far ahead, Dave.

00:44:54.858 --> 00:44:58.962
- (laughs) Do you get sick of
talking about the race thing?

00:44:58.962 --> 00:45:00.130
Because I don't know

00:45:00.130 --> 00:45:01.231 align:start size:88% position:13%
how much you know
about me, specifically,

00:45:01.231 --> 00:45:02.599
but I was a progressive.

00:45:02.599 --> 00:45:05.569
I was a lefty, and then, really,

00:45:05.569 --> 00:45:07.304 align:start size:84% position:16%
there was a few things
that woke me up,

00:45:07.304 --> 00:45:09.506 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I would say the one thing
that I really shifted on

00:45:09.506 --> 00:45:11.341
was libertarian economics,

00:45:11.341 --> 00:45:12.543
talking to Thomas Sowell

00:45:12.543 --> 00:45:14.978 align:start size:84% position:16%
and some of the other
great libertarian

00:45:14.978 --> 00:45:17.548
and conservative economists,

00:45:17.548 --> 00:45:19.683
so I could talk to
you about economy,

00:45:19.683 --> 00:45:21.685
the economy all day long,
and that would be great,

00:45:21.685 --> 00:45:23.954
but of course, we gotta
do some of this stuff.

00:45:23.954 --> 00:45:24.788
Do you ever just,

00:45:25.956 --> 00:45:28.092
when you're talking to
John on the podcast,

00:45:28.092 --> 00:45:30.894
do you ever just get sick
of having to do that thing?

00:45:32.329 --> 00:45:33.597
- Yeah, in fact, John and
I have talked about it.

00:45:33.597 --> 00:45:35.132
It's in the archives somewhere.

00:45:35.132 --> 00:45:38.035
We've had a couple of episodes
where we've just reflected on

00:45:38.035 --> 00:45:39.403
why don't we get out
of the race business?

00:45:39.403 --> 00:45:42.573
He's a linguist, and
he's a very talented

00:45:42.573 --> 00:45:45.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
and accomplished
professor of linguistics,

00:45:45.342 --> 00:45:47.077
writes books about the stuff.

00:45:47.077 --> 00:45:48.612
I'm a technical economist.

00:45:48.612 --> 00:45:51.515 align:start size:97% position:3%
I have the accolades to
show, and so forth and so on,

00:45:51.515 --> 00:45:54.852 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we could live
perfectly fine, quiet lives

00:45:54.852 --> 00:45:56.353
just doing our thing,

00:45:57.454 --> 00:45:59.456
so yeah, we've talked about it.

00:45:59.456 --> 00:46:02.059
I even tried it back in the day,

00:46:02.059 --> 00:46:05.929
back when I was a
Reagan Republican,

00:46:05.929 --> 00:46:08.599 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I was on my
way to Washington,

00:46:08.599 --> 00:46:11.101 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I ended up getting
caught in a scandal

00:46:11.101 --> 00:46:13.103
with a extramarital affair,

00:46:13.103 --> 00:46:15.739
and I got accused of
assaulting a woman,

00:46:15.739 --> 00:46:16.974
which I did not do,

00:46:16.974 --> 00:46:19.777
but it was the end
of my public career

00:46:20.711 --> 00:46:22.279
for that period,

00:46:22.279 --> 00:46:24.615
and I said, (sighs) "Let me
get out of the race business.

00:46:24.615 --> 00:46:25.549
"Let me go back,"

00:46:25.549 --> 00:46:26.984
and I published three papers

00:46:26.984 --> 00:46:29.486 align:start size:94% position:6%
in "The American Economic
Review" in one year,

00:46:29.486 --> 00:46:32.723 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is virtually an
unprecedented accomplishment

00:46:33.590 --> 00:46:35.325
because I put my head down,

00:46:35.325 --> 00:46:37.561
and just started
doing my research.

00:46:37.561 --> 00:46:40.364
It lasted for maybe
three or four years,

00:46:40.364 --> 00:46:42.766 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the next thing you
knew, the events of the day

00:46:42.766 --> 00:46:44.368
kept pulling me back
and pulling me back,

00:46:44.368 --> 00:46:47.104
and I finally just
stopped fighting it.

00:46:47.104 --> 00:46:49.306 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think this is
part of my calling.

00:46:49.306 --> 00:46:51.742
It's not the whole thing,
but it's a part of it.

00:46:51.742 --> 00:46:55.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm doing some stuff that
relatively few people

00:46:55.012 --> 00:46:57.881
will/can do,

00:46:57.881 --> 00:46:58.749
and it's important.

00:46:58.749 --> 00:47:00.450
I think it's important,

00:47:00.450 --> 00:47:03.187
so I've kind of acquiesced

00:47:03.187 --> 00:47:05.589
to that being a part of my role,

00:47:05.589 --> 00:47:09.626 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I like reading
novels, and philosophy,

00:47:09.626 --> 00:47:11.061
and history, and whatnot.

00:47:11.061 --> 00:47:13.564 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is not my whole
life, but it is what I'm,

00:47:13.564 --> 00:47:15.732 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's the beat that I'm
covering right now.

00:47:15.732 --> 00:47:17.467
- So I got one one more for you,

00:47:17.467 --> 00:47:19.002
and I think I know
the answer already

00:47:19.002 --> 00:47:21.138
'cause you're saying a lot of
this with a smile on your face

00:47:21.138 --> 00:47:22.639 align:start size:84% position:16%
and just sort of your
general demeanor,

00:47:22.639 --> 00:47:24.808
but are you hopeful that we
can get through this thing

00:47:24.808 --> 00:47:27.344 align:start size:84% position:16%
and get back to some
of the principles

00:47:27.344 --> 00:47:28.579
that you've talked about here

00:47:28.579 --> 00:47:30.147
that you think are the
right guiding principles

00:47:30.147 --> 00:47:31.381
for this country?

00:47:31.381 --> 00:47:32.883
You think we can
get through this?

00:47:32.883 --> 00:47:35.786
- I don't know. I alternate.

00:47:35.786 --> 00:47:37.988
Sometimes I'm very pessimistic.

00:47:37.988 --> 00:47:40.724 align:start position:0%
What's happening in our schools
is absolutely fundamental.

00:47:40.724 --> 00:47:42.192
I'm talking about K through 12.

00:47:42.192 --> 00:47:43.427 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm saying what
we're teaching kids,

00:47:43.427 --> 00:47:47.264
the 1619 Project scared
the bejesus out of me.

00:47:47.264 --> 00:47:48.732
That's "The New York Times."

00:47:48.732 --> 00:47:50.467
That's a rewriting
of American history

00:47:50.467 --> 00:47:52.903
centering it on the
fact of slavery,

00:47:52.903 --> 00:47:56.573
and I don't mind, of course,
that we would teach our kids

00:47:56.573 --> 00:47:59.209 align:start size:88% position:13%
about the history of
slavery in this country,

00:47:59.209 --> 00:48:02.145
but 1776,

00:48:02.145 --> 00:48:05.849 align:start size:84% position:16%
1787, the Constitution
being ratified,

00:48:05.849 --> 00:48:10.420 align:start size:97% position:3%
man, that is a world historic
creation of a government

00:48:10.420 --> 00:48:13.824
under the ideas of
the Enlightenment

00:48:13.824 --> 00:48:17.861
with real institutions that
have worked for centuries

00:48:17.861 --> 00:48:20.397
and been a beacon to the world.

00:48:20.397 --> 00:48:24.201
You don't put 1619 next to 1776

00:48:24.201 --> 00:48:25.602
and call it a draw.

00:48:26.803 --> 00:48:29.673
That's throwing away the
baby with the bathwater.

00:48:29.673 --> 00:48:32.242 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's a disaster
if that kind of idea

00:48:32.242 --> 00:48:34.578
gets in the heads of too
many of our young people,

00:48:34.578 --> 00:48:36.546
so I think the jury is out,

00:48:36.546 --> 00:48:40.350
and I think the battlefield
is K through 12 education.

00:48:40.350 --> 00:48:43.020 align:start size:84% position:16%
Critical race theory
in the schools,

00:48:43.020 --> 00:48:44.721
now, there is free speech.

00:48:44.721 --> 00:48:46.890 align:start size:84% position:16%
We could fight about
creation science.

00:48:46.890 --> 00:48:48.859
We could fight about sex
education in the schools,

00:48:48.859 --> 00:48:51.061
so I guess we're gonna have
to fight about this too,

00:48:51.061 --> 00:48:52.896
but to me, that's ground zero.

00:48:54.231 --> 00:48:56.833 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Glenn, it's been a
pleasure. This was overdue.

00:48:56.833 --> 00:48:58.802
I thank you for taking the time.

00:48:58.802 --> 00:49:00.103
- My pleasure, Dave.

00:49:00.103 --> 00:49:01.605
- If you're looking

